<article class="env-lemma" id="0E6R">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0E6R">Lemma <span data-tag="0E6R">109.19.3</span>.</a> Let $X \to S$ be a family of curves with Gorenstein fibres equidimensional of dimension $1$ (Lemma <a href="/tag/0E6M" data-tag="0E6M">109.12.2</a>). Then the relative dualizing sheaf $\omega _{X/S}$ is an invertible $\mathcal{O}_ X$-module whose formation commutes with arbitrary base change. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      This is true because the pullback of the relative dualizing module to a fibre is invertible by the discussion above. Alternatively, you can argue exactly as in the proof of Lemma <a href="/tag/0E6P" data-tag="0E6P">109.19.1</a> and deduce the result from Duality for Schemes, Lemma <a href="/tag/0C08" data-tag="0C08">48.25.10</a>. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>